hello today we are going to talk about  decision trees and random forest  decision tree is a type of a  classification algorithm  each decision tree is created by rules  binary means the branch that can be  either yes or no and if the expression  of a gene is higher than x the classes  will separate  for example in the figure here you can  see that the luminance class can be  separated from all the other classes  based on the gene  e n s g triple zero triple zero 1 6 2 9.  this rest of the samples are further  divided into the other classes based on  other genes that are separating this  group into cloud and low normal like and  basic classes the algorithm will  continue to run until it has effectively  separated all the samples into some  group  if we use less genes in the training  data set we will get different results  because the data set can be dealt with  differently based on available features  in the second example of a binary  decision tree for breast cancer gene  expression table we see the data is  separated into three groups luminal  basal and cloudine low with only two  genes  binary decision trees are initiated in a  random start so the genes selected in  the procedure can change from time to  time  to try running decision tree pipeline on  the server select the supervised  learning section and area of analysis  where you are going to upload the  training and the test data and select  the decision trees button in the  pipeline builder  so let's get started  upload your data  and now  once the upload is finished select your  train  and test data  now click on start  and click on decision tree  now end your pipeline give a name to  your pipeline  and  stay updated with the status of your  pipeline timely by checking the  pipelines  in the results you will see the  prediction for test set and a pdf with  the decision tree visualization  also you would obtain a confusion matrix  that reveals an issue that the algorithm  ran into cloud and low was identified  four times as cloud and low and three  times as normal like that's the  confusion  if we look at the expression of these  genes we will see that they in fact do  look confusing the first gene is fairly  good luminal samples have a consistently  higher expression than the rest of the  samples for this gene  but for the second gene it is not a good  classifier  to learn more about decision trees and  tandem forest and how they work what are  the algorithms and what other results  that you are going to obtain and how do  you interpret it  visit  lesson number 14 of transcriptomics  course on  learn.amixlogic.com portal
 

